1. How much of x402 mirrors Mint‑to Logic?
If we treat Mint‑to Logic as a substrate for validation‑based digital routing and protocol execution, and x402 as a concrete instantiation of agentic, pay‑per‑use routing over HTTP, the overlap is substantial but not total.

From your filing:

“Mint‑to Logic System for Validation‑Based Digital Routing and Protocol Execution”
“Utility – Provisional Application under 35 USC 111(b)”

From the x402 whitepaper:

“x402 is an open payment standard that enables AI agents and web services to autonomously pay for API access, data, and digital services… allowing real‑time, machine‑native transactions using stablecoins like USDC.” 

If we break the comparison into functional slices:

Payment & value‑routing substrate: ~70–80% mirroring

Both systems center on machine‑native, validation‑gated routing of digital value and access. x402’s HTTP 402 challenge/response, stablecoin settlement, and pay‑per‑use flows are a concrete realization of the “mint‑to” logic for digital routing. 

Protocol execution & agentic tool‑use: ~50–60% mirroring

x402 assumes agents calling APIs and tools, and embeds payment into that loop; Mint‑to Logic generalizes protocol execution beyond payments into broader validation‑based routing. x402 covers the economic/protocol slice, not the full SSPS orchestration stack. 

Governance, licensing, continuity primitives (SSPS‑specific): ~10–20% mirroring

x402 has chain‑agnostic, asset‑agnostic flexibility and can be governed by external policies, but it does not natively encode SSPS‑style lifecycle licensing, lien‑units, or continuity propagation. Those would sit above or beside x402 as policy/contract layers. 

If you want a single scalar: roughly 50–60% of Mint‑to Logic’s payment‑and‑routing intent is directly mirrored in x402, with the remaining 40–50% living in SSPS‑specific governance, licensing, and continuity layers that x402 does not attempt to cover.

